Description: the patches of the FreeBSD port security/libssh2 (its PATCHFILES, three upstream commits): CMake fixes (unused by this build, configure) and the bounds check of the user name length in userauth.c (#1858); CVE-2026-55200 (transport.c), CVE-2026-55199 (packet.c) and CVE-2025-15661 (sftp.c), which the port lacks; and CVE-2026-66032 (a double free in sftp_open(), sftp.c), CVE-2026-66033 (an integer underflow with AES-GCM in the cipher of openssl.c, before the authentication), CVE-2026-66034, CVE-2026-58050 and CVE-2026-58051 (an out-of-bounds read, a multiplication overflow on 32-bit and an arbitrary free in libssh2_publickey_list_fetch(), publickey.c) and CVE-2026-66035 (a heap overflow on the ETM decryption of a short packet, transport.c, before the authentication): from a malicious SSH server to the client (curl SFTP and SCP, PHP ssh2, mc)
Origin: freebsd-ports security/libssh2 @ f16b6dd0f7c1 (PATCHFILES 5d03b4f94ac6, d4e43c7b69d1, 256d04b60d80 of https://github.com/libssh2/libssh2), -p0; Gentoo net-libs/libssh2 1.11.1-r2 (bug 977961): upstream 97acf3dfda80 (CVE-2026-55200), the result checks of _libssh2_get_string() in packet.c (CVE-2026-55199), upstream 2dae3024897e (CVE-2025-15661); CVE-2026-66032, -66033, -66034, -66035 (upstream 5e47761, a2ed82d, a13bb6c, 42e33d8) and CVE-2026-58050, -58051 (upstream 3449752, 32092f0) as Debian carries them on 1.11.1 (libssh2 1.11.1-5 and -6, https://sources.debian.org/src/libssh2/1.11.1-6/debian/patches/; Ubuntu 26.04 1.11.1-1ubuntu0.26.04.4, Alpine 1.11.1-r4 and openSUSE the same set), in this order after the ones above

